WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Did you know that there’s a Taco Bell at&nbsp;
the Pentagon? Technically, we can no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.680
confirm it, but back before the information got&nbsp;
restricted there were websites confirming that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.720
yup. There was, in fact, an entire food&nbsp;
court there. Complete with Subway, a KFC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.840
Dunkin’ Donuts, and, of course, the Bell.&nbsp;
Gotta say, bold choice there US Military,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.680
BOLD CHOICE. Hope your toilets are as&nbsp;
powerful as your fighter jets. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.680
none of this has anything to do with today’s&nbsp;
theory. It was just an interesting thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.760
I learned while researching for it that&nbsp;
I wanted to pass along to you. Ah, I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:35.440
no good transition here, so ah, THROW UP A TITLE&nbsp;
CARD! make it EXPLODE and HYPER MASCULINE y’know.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.320
Hello Internet! Welcome to&nbsp;
Film Theory. Talk to me Goose.&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.080
*HONK!*
So, I’ve a confession to make;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.840
I do not like the original Top Gun. I know…I&nbsp;
know, It is cinematic blasphemy. But the cocky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.120
hotshot protagonist is just too unlikable, the&nbsp;
treatment of women is too outdated, and overall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.360
everyone is just too darn sweaty. Seriously. It’s&nbsp;
like they jumped in a pool before filming every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.400
scene. Someone PLEASE get these men some towels!
The only reason I’m telling you this is to set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.160
context. Because, despite my lowest of low&nbsp;
expectations, I LOVED Top Gun: Maverick!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.400
This story of a few fighter pilots trying to&nbsp;
accomplish an impossible mission had me at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:18.560
edge of my seat the entire time, in a way that no&nbsp;
world-ending sky beam ever has in the last decade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:24.240
It was exciting, but not overdone. It was simple,&nbsp;
but it wasn’t dumb. And it was nostalgic, but, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.760
like annoying levels of nostalgic. It was a movie&nbsp;
that was absolutely cheesy, but it recognized that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.120
fact and embraced the cheese. Gotta say, I just&nbsp;
really really liked it. And apparently I’m not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.680
the only one. In a year that so far has featured&nbsp;
massive MCU crossovers, the return of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.240
popular superhero ever, and the conclusion to&nbsp;
one of the 90’s most popular movie franchises…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:50.080
the biggest film story of 2022 has been Top&nbsp;
Gun: Maverick taking home Box Office Gold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:56.560
crossing a billion dollars and crushing absolutely&nbsp;
ALL of the competition. Like, it’s not even close.&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.800
But perhaps, most interesting of all is that it&nbsp;
does all of this without an identifiable “bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.960
guy.” Despite setting up this hugely important&nbsp;
and impossible mission from the word “Go,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.040
Maverick never officially identifies the&nbsp;
“hostile enemy nation” that Tom Cruise and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:14.080
company end up in aerial combat against. We&nbsp;
never actually get to SEE the enemy’s face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.760
And this is actually a tradition with the&nbsp;
franchise. You see, in the original Top Gun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.760
they did the same thing…they never once identify&nbsp;
who they’re supposed to be flying against. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:26.400
most audiences generally assumed that the enemy&nbsp;
was Soviet Russia, provided that the Cold War was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:32.240
still pretty hot in 1986 and the fighters are ID’d&nbsp;
as “MiGs” – aka jets manufactured by the Russian:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.520
Mikoyan and Gurevich Design Bureau, but in&nbsp;
reality, according to the filmmakers years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:43.040
the intent was for the enemy nation to be NORTH&nbsp;
KOREA! Yeah. I was surprised too. Apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.480
the only reason this became less clear&nbsp;
in the final film was because the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.080
State Department was trying to “normalize&nbsp;
relations” with the North Korean government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.640
so they asked Top Gun’s producers to make the&nbsp;
bad guys some unidentified “hostiles” flying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.160
some made-up jets. By the way, that relationship&nbsp;
between the government and Hollywood…yeah…probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:02.720
worth some theories one day.
Anyway, in the original Top Gun, this didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.720
really make all that much of a difference, it&nbsp;
was only an issue at the beginning and end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.840
story. The enemy nation was just a minor bookend&nbsp;
for a plot about hotshots working with each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.880
at school. In the new movie Maverick, though,&nbsp;
this whole plot is built on an impossible suicide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:22.560
mission to stop a country from achieving nuclear&nbsp;
power. And since this is a realistic-ish drama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.960
you can’t just cheat like a Marvel movie and make&nbsp;
up a fake country! So, to keep the enemy presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.760
anonymous, Top Gun: Maverick keeps the terrain&nbsp;
remote and interchangeable; it largely confines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:35.680
the action to flight suits and cockpits; and it&nbsp;
makes the mission at the center of this whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.280
thing covert, on a need to know basis only. But&nbsp;
here’s the thing I NEED to know! And I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.280
there’s enough in here to figure it out. While&nbsp;
most of the details are kept vague and nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:50.160
is explicitly stated, we do learn A LOT IMPLICITLY&nbsp;
about this nation during the course of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.880
training and final execution of the mission. So I&nbsp;
wanted to see if we could figure this thing out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:59.440
What is the “rogue nation” that Tom Cruise and the&nbsp;
gang are fighting against in Top Gun: Maverick?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:03.840
Honestly, I walked out of the theater thinking…yet&nbsp;
again…”that was a thinly-veiled reference to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.880
Russia” Spoiler alert: It’s not. In fact, that&nbsp;
initial assumption is SO wrong! So today, we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.840
entering the Danger Zone of international politics&nbsp;
to see whether or not we can piece together the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.040
faceless enemy of Top Gun: Maverick using context&nbsp;
clues, geographic hints, and easter eggs that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.360
sprinkled throughout the film. Sure, Tom Cruise,&nbsp;
you can sneak past an onslaught of surface-to-air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.320
missiles, but you can’t sneak past MatPat.&nbsp;
Who are Maverick, Rooster, and their wingmen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.200
REALLY up against in the new movie? Strap in&nbsp;
friends, and check your six, we’re going in!&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.360
As I just mentioned, despite the enemy being kept&nbsp;
anonymous throughout the new movie, we actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.360
learn a lot about them over the course of the&nbsp;
film. Here’s the set-up: A uranium-enrichment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.600
facility in an unnamed foreign territory&nbsp;
is very close to achieving nuclear weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:50.000
and the Navy does NOT want this country to&nbsp;
have nukes. To help bring down the facility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.440
the Navy calls in Maverick, Tom Cruise’s cocky&nbsp;
all-star pilot, to teach a team of elite graduates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.080
how to accomplish this dangerous bombing mission.&nbsp;
The mission involves a low-altitude run through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:04.560
treacherous canyon defended by missiles, followed&nbsp;
by a one-in-a-million shot at a distant target,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.480
and then a daring escape that will almost&nbsp;
certainly involve aerial dogfights against at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:12.960
least THREE “fifth generation” enemy fighters.&nbsp;
Basically, this thing is like the Death Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:17.680
trench run from Star Wars but with actual fighter&nbsp;
jets instead of spacecraft. During the finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.160
for both maximum closure AND maximum nostalgia,&nbsp;
when both Maverick and Rooster (the son of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:26.480
Maverick’s dead best friend Goose) are shot down&nbsp;
behind enemy lines, they’re forced to fight their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:33.280
way home in a stolen vintage F-14 Tomcat… just&nbsp;
like the team flew back in the original in 1986.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:38.080
The whole thing ends with the crew overcoming&nbsp;
the odds…and their own personal daddy issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.920
all with a minimal amount of sweat. So,&nbsp;
hug it out there gentlemen! you deserve it.&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.760
First off, let’s look at the mission parameters&nbsp;
themselves. We’re told that the unnamed hostile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:50.320
actor at the center of this bombing raid is&nbsp;
a “Rogue Nation” in the year 2022. Now, funny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.560
enough, the State Department officially STOPPED&nbsp;
using that term decades ago, instead replacing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:58.560
it with the much less exciting label “States&nbsp;
of Concern.” Something tells me, though, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:02.800
wasn’t a mistake on the part of the writers, but&nbsp;
rather an intentional reference to a certain other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.000
Tom Cruise franchise. We’re also told that this&nbsp;
nation’s work to enrich uranium is “in violation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.320
of a number of treaties”, and that the enrichment&nbsp;
facility needs to be destroyed BEFORE they can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:17.040
the uranium to the point where they can create&nbsp;
nuclear weapons. Which means that they DO NOT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.760
currently have nukes. What they DO have though&nbsp;
is a multi-tiered air and ground arsenal that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:27.280
includes older refurbished aircraft like the F-14,&nbsp;
a defense array of Soviet-era S125 surface-to-air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:32.560
missiles, AND new fifth-generation fighters.&nbsp;
Specifically, that’s a Russian Sukhoi SU-57,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:37.920
with its real, actual NATO reporting name being -&nbsp;
get this - “FELON”. We also know from digital maps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.080
and training simulations that we see in the movie,&nbsp;
that the terrain is mountainous with at least one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:46.400
river and a large bridge, and that it’s within&nbsp;
flying range of the ocean since the Navy fighters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:51.040
are able to disembark directly from an aircraft&nbsp;
carrier. And while some convenient cloud-cover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:54.960
prevents us from seeing what the coastline of this&nbsp;
country actually looks like, we definitely get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.520
VERY clear look at the very real terrain thanks&nbsp;
to our heroes flying low and having to hoof it on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:03.760
foot after getting shot down. Here, we see&nbsp;
that at least part of the region is snowy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:07.920
meaning that we’re either at a high altitude&nbsp;
or that it’s seasonally cold. Looking at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.560
plant life in the region, we can see large pine&nbsp;
and fir trees. In reality, that’s because these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:16.800
scenes were mainly shot in Washington State’s&nbsp;
Cascade Mountains, so, you know, part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.120
country that we’re ultimately looking for is going&nbsp;
to have vibes similar to the Pacific Northwest.&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.120
Like I said, that’s actually a surprising&nbsp;
amount of detail to work with! So, to start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:30.240
let’s explore the term “Rogue Nation” or “Rogue&nbsp;
State”. Currently, there are only eight countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.480
that are officially categorized as “Rogue States”&nbsp;
by the US Government, with a ninth that kinda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:39.600
makes it on the list by default. On the list,&nbsp;
we have Venezuela, Syria, Russia, Nicaragua,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:45.280
North Korea, Iran, Cuba and Belarus, with that&nbsp;
ninth country being Afghanistan since its current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:49.280
government is controlled by the Taliban who&nbsp;
aren’t recognized as legitimate by the US.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:53.360
Alos, just to mention it for completionists sake:&nbsp;
I’m gonna include China as a candidate here on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:58.160
list for the movie ONLY. Because, while officially&nbsp;
on “friendly” terms with the US, the two countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.320
do have significant geopolitical disagreements.
So already we’ve narrowed the list of possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:07.760
contenders down from 195 countries in the world,&nbsp;
to only ten. And we can actually knock off a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:12.480
few more quickly based on the mission parameters.&nbsp;
Right off the bat, it can’t be China, North Korea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:17.200
or Russia since all of those countries already&nbsp;
have themselves nuclear weapons. So them building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:22.320
a NEW enrichment facility wouldn’t be as big of&nbsp;
a deal, or as top of a secret as we see the enemy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:26.240
nation in Maverick making of it. So now, we’re&nbsp;
down to seven. From here, we’re gonna have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:30.320
dig a bit deeper into the details for each&nbsp;
candidate. Afghanistan certainly LOOKS the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:35.440
part. It has mountainous territory, snow, forests,&nbsp;
defensible canyons, just like we see in the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:40.080
Afghanistan is also crawling with Soviet-era&nbsp;
weaponry, and let’s just say that the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.880
leadership would be very unlikely to decline&nbsp;
having their own uranium enrichment facility. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:50.000
of it would seem to fit. Except, there’s just one&nbsp;
problem… Afghanistan is landlocked, approximately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.320
280 miles away from its nearest coast in&nbsp;
the Arabian Sea, so that’s going to strain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:59.680
the logistical possibility of the film’s finale.
The “no coastline” issue also eliminates Belarus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:03.440
despite its Russian-aligned leader DEFINITELY&nbsp;
being on America’s naughty list these days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:07.520
Another point against Belarus; though its climate&nbsp;
is in line with what we see in the film, its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:12.480
geography is actually too flat for the mountains&nbsp;
we see in the film. On the flip side is Cuba,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:16.800
which we’re going to eliminate for the exact&nbsp;
opposite problem. While it’s absolutely close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.120
enough to ocean water, Cuba is DEFINITELY&nbsp;
not our culprit. It’s a tropical island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:26.480
and therefore doesn’t have any snow. This is&nbsp;
also going to eliminate Nicaragua from the list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:30.640
since it doesn’t ever snow there either. We&nbsp;
can also eliminate Venezuela - though it snows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:34.400
a LITTLE up in the highest elevations of the&nbsp;
Andes mountains, it definitely doesn’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:39.440
a tree canopy that looks anything like what we&nbsp;
see in Maverick. The Andes are a Tropical Montane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:44.720
Cloud Forest with a flora coverage primarily of&nbsp;
conifer trees, which would NOT match the Cascade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:48.640
Mountain aesthetic shooting location at all.
And just like that, our roster has suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:55.280
narrowed from 195 to only two: Syria and Iran.&nbsp;
And we did that in what, like a page? a page and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.280 --> 00:10:00.560
a half? two long-ish paragraphs? That’s gotta be a&nbsp;
record here. This is practically a YouTube Short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:04.720
we’re being so efficient. Anyway, Syria and&nbsp;
Iran. These two countries are actually pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:09.280
close together geographically, both would be&nbsp;
accessible from the Persian Gulf using the F-18&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:13.520
Hornet fighter jets that we see our heroes flying&nbsp;
in Maverick. And while this may be a surprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:18.080
given that Middle Eastern countries often are&nbsp;
stereotyped as giant deserts, they both also have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:23.360
mountainous regions where the snow and vegetation&nbsp;
could reasonably pass the Pacific Northwest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:28.720
Both countries have also been either suspected or&nbsp;
accused of developing nuclear programs outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:33.120
international agreements in the past, so at least&nbsp;
for the purposes of the fictitious story of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:37.040
movie, that part is also going to check out.
Of the pair, Syria seems like it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:41.280
most logical answer. In fact, this whole&nbsp;
movie’s plot may just be based on a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:46.480
operation that actually happened there. You see,&nbsp;
Syria has already attempted to enrich Uranium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:52.160
in the recent past, starting a secret project&nbsp;
at a reactor in a remote valley back in 2007.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:57.120
Ha…secret enrichment program in a remote valley&nbsp;
you say? Someone call in Tom Cruise! The facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:01.760
was eventually blown up by Israeli fighter jets&nbsp;
in a mission that was only acknowledged a decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:07.280
later as “Operation Outside the Box”. Gotta love&nbsp;
how militaries worldwide name their top secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:11.520
operations… Anyway, the circumstances of that&nbsp;
event are so similar to the mission in Maverick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:15.840
I wouldn’t be surprised if at least part of the&nbsp;
plot’s inspiration originated here. It’s also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.320
worth noting that the Syrian Government has had&nbsp;
far more active military engagements recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:24.480
its President is also know for buying&nbsp;
Russian Arms and military support. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:28.640
if anyone’s going to have the Russian-built fifth&nbsp;
generation fighters like we see in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:33.040
Syria is one where it’s going to make a lot of&nbsp;
sense. In fact, Russia has already tested their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:37.920
fifth-generation fighters over Syria multiple&nbsp;
times in the past. Syria also has the much older&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:43.360
Sukhoi SU-22 and -24 models as part of their&nbsp;
active air force. So really it seems like Syria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:47.520
is the obvious slam dunk here: They’ve got the&nbsp;
planes, they’ve got the geography, they’ve got the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:52.720
history of trying to enrich the uranium. It seems&nbsp;
that the only thing they don’t have are the F-14s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:56.880
They certainly have a fair number of older planes&nbsp;
in their air force, but they’re missing that one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:02.480
crucial model. And it’s a model that we HAVE to&nbsp;
have to get that big nostalgic punch in the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:07.120
act of the movie. How else are two testosterone&nbsp;
charged men supposed to overcome their beef!?&nbsp;

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:12.240
So, that just leaves us with one: Iran. And&nbsp;
they check pretty much all of our boxes. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:16.640
they haven’t had as many recent active military&nbsp;
entanglements, they DO have a similar history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:21.920
of buying up Russian aircraft and weaponry. Like&nbsp;
Syria, they do have a Sukhoi Su-24 in their air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:26.400
force’s active roster…again, a much older model,&nbsp;
but it shows a history of purchasing Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:31.200
supplies. And recently, it seems that they’ve&nbsp;
expressed interest in purchasing Russia’s newest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:35.520
fifth generation fighters. Additionally, Iran&nbsp;
has similar terrain and geography to what we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:39.440
in the film, they’re close enough to an ocean&nbsp;
that a US Carriers could launch missiles and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:44.320
jet-fighters into it for a mission, AND they’ve&nbsp;
had nuclear ambitions in the recent past. See,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:48.480
though Iran was supposed to denuclearize thanks&nbsp;
to a deal made during the Obama Administration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:53.280
the program has entered much more unknown&nbsp;
territory after more recent presidencies withdrew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:56.960
the US from the agreement. Of more immediate&nbsp;
importance to the events of Maverick, though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:04.240
Iran is one of the ONLY modern military powers&nbsp;
still operating an active fleet of F-14 Tomcats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:08.080
That means, it’s just about the only plausible&nbsp;
candidate on the list that could also allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:13.040
for that nostalgia-rific climax with Tom Cruise&nbsp;
and Rooster flying the original “Top Gun Jet.”&nbsp;

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:17.680
And there you have it, Loyal Theorists. By process&nbsp;
of elimination, the most likely candidate for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:22.880
unnamed enemy in Top Gun: Maverick is Iran. It’s&nbsp;
the only place that has the right mix of politics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:28.000
military resources, geographic location,&nbsp;
topographical details, environmental terrain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:31.680
and specific use of weapons technology&nbsp;
to make the events of the film play out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:35.360
exactly like we see them do on screen.
Or, you know what? Maybe I’m completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:40.640
wrong. Maybe the big twist of this whole thing&nbsp;
is that really this movie was a secret crossover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:45.440
Just look at the enemy’s logo on the fighter&nbsp;
jets. Looks an awful lot like THIS symbol;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:51.520
the symbol of ARSTOZKA from the game “PAPERS&nbsp;
PLEASE!” Maybe the real theory here is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:57.040
Top Gun: Maverick was actually the most&nbsp;
successful video game movie of all time.&nbsp;

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:15.840
But hey, that’s just a theory!&nbsp;
A Film Theory! Aaaaand CUT!

